WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.200
It may actually be an understatement to call
Matt Damon a prolific actor.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.680
He's in dozens of movies, including "Good
Will Hunting," "Saving Private Ryan," and

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.049
the "Bourne" franchise.

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:12.299
He's long been considered one of the most
successful actors in Hollywood, thanks to

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:16.140
his impressive range, likable screen presence,
and other talents — don't forget that he

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.990
wrote "Good Will Hunting" with Ben Affleck.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.699
“How do you like them apples?"

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.199
Damon has endured some rough patches, of course,
but he remains an integral part of the film

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:25.199
industry.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:29.539
Back in 2007, Damon was named Hollywood's
most bankable star, with the CBC reporting,

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:34.239
"For every dollar he was paid for his last
three roles, Damon brought in $29 U.S. of

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:35.239
gross income."

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.260
Aside from his many great films, Damon has
been part of several projects that didn't

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.329
quite live up to expectations.

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:45.850
From flops like "Suburbicon" to movies that
got mostly average reviews, such as "Titan

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.350
A.E.," Damon has seen every side of a movie's
possible critical reception.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.059
Thankfully for him, one of his less popular
movies, a wartime action thriller based on

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:57.500
a nonfiction book, is getting another chance
on Netflix — and so far, it's staying high

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.879
on the streaming platform's trending list.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.699
The 2010 movie "Green Zone" was inspired by
the book "Imperial Life in the Emerald City,"

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:07.320
written by journalist Rajiv Chandrasekaran.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.090
The book describes what it was like within
the Green Zone, the International Zone of

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.850
Baghdad, during the 2003 U.S. invasion of
Iraq, and in the subsequent transfer of power.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.710
"Green Zone," which reunites Damon with "Bourne
Supremacy" and "Bourne Ultimatum" director

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.930
Paul Greengrass, follows U.S. Army Chief Warrant
Officer Roy Miller, played by Damon, who realizes

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:29.110
that the intelligence the Pentagon is receiving
is faulty, as all of their leads for weapons

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.450
of mass destruction end up going nowhere.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.810
Miller decides to take things into his own
hands and begins hunting for WMD on his own,

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.329
while uncovering some harsh truths along the
way.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:43.180
"Green Zone" won over about half of critics,
with a rating of 53% on Rotten Tomatoes, but

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.640
it was considered a flop at the box office.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.630
Some critics said that "Green Zone" strayed
a bit too far from the truth, but others appreciated

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.720
its commentary on the motivation behind the
war in Iraq.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.230
Andrew O'Hagan of The Evening Standard went
so far as to call it "one of the best war

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:57.259
films ever made."

00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:58.259
He added,

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:02.229
"It exposed the terrible lies that stood behind
the decision of the U.S. and Britain to prosecute

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:07.220
the war, and it does so in a way that is dramatically
brilliant, morally complex and relentlessly

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.220
thrilling."

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.911
The majority of recent viewers have positive
things to say about "Green Zone," with some

00:02:11.911 --> 00:02:14.610
taking to social media to offer their compliments.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.440
One Twitter user said they consider it to
be an "underrated action movie," and yet another

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.480
confessed that they "enjoyed it more than
[they] expected to."

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.100
Others have commented on how "Green Zone"
shines a light on the conspiracies surrounding

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.490
the war in Iraq, adding that Damon's stellar
performance introduces another layer of excitement.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:32.790
"You wanna sit around and dig holes all day?

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.580
I wanna get something done."

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:40.200
Audience critiques on Rotten Tomatoes also
mostly lean positive, with almost all in agreement

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.750
that Damon's acting is a strong point of the
film.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.070
One reviewer succinctly said,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.151
"I'm a fan of Matt Damon, he is perfectly
satisfactory in the role of Roy Miller; he

00:02:48.151 --> 00:02:49.450
holds things together."

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.030
When asked about how accurate the film actually
is during a 2010 interview, Damon told Collider

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.640
that some real-life people had to be boiled
down to one central character to make the

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
story work better.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.640
He said:

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.040
"There were no weapons in Iraq.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.040
That's true.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.830
A lot of people went there thinking there
were.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.950
That's true, too.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.370
No one's really explained what the f--- happened.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.370
That's true too.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.980
[...] I mean, what we have in the movie is,
yeah, we have a guy playing kind of a neocon

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.220
type who actually invented a source, right?

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.070
So that's not based on anything, that's obviously
something that, for the movie, for the kind

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.370
of architecture of a thriller to work, it
needs to boil down to a couple characters."

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.370
Fans of the "Bourne" franchise's action-packed
plot lines will likely enjoy seeing Damon

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.060
in another thrilling role, while history and
politics buffs will appreciate the social

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.950
commentary of "Green Zone."

